1
00:00:00,832 --> 00:00:09,783
Welcome to the North America capitals, episode one of five. First, I clear the
chosen names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,441 --> 00:00:19,566
We begin with the three great capitals of the north: Ottawa and Washington,
and then Mexico City, far to the south of them.

3
00:00:20,237 --> 00:00:22,071
Let's bring Ottawa into view.

4
00:00:27,806 --> 00:00:37,106
Ottawa stands on the Ottawa river in eastern Ontario, right at the border with
Quebec, where the river meets the Rideau canal and the hills beyond.

5
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:51,065
From the Algonquin adawe, "to trade", for the river that was a trading route:
Queen Victoria chose it as capital in 1857, partly because it was far from the
American border.

6
00:00:51,722 --> 00:01:01,405
Remember Ottawa as the trade-route capital, picked to sit far from the
American border, whose frozen Rideau canal becomes the world's largest skating
rink.

7
00:01:02,077 --> 00:01:06,002
Let's bring Washington, D.C. into view.

8
00:01:08,182 --> 00:01:16,239
Washington stands on the Potomac river, on the Atlantic side of the
Appalachian mountains, between Maryland and Virginia.

9
00:01:16,896 --> 00:01:35,040
George Washington chose the site but never named it after himself; the
commissioners laying it out did, in 1791, and the District of Columbia is a
Latin flourish for the same Columbus. It is in no state on purpose, so the
capital would never be beholden to one.

10
00:01:35,704 --> 00:01:46,536
Remember Washington as the capital that belongs to no state, and the city that
stays low because no building may rise above the limit set in 1910.

11
00:01:47,205 --> 00:01:50,142
Let's bring Mexico City into view.

12
00:01:52,838 --> 00:02:01,882
Mexico City stands in the Valley of Mexico, ringed by volcanoes, at about
2,240 metres above the sea.

13
00:02:02,533 --> 00:02:15,374
From Mexica, the Aztecs' own name for themselves, so the city and the country
share it. It is built on the drained lake bed of Tenochtitlán and sinks up to
50 cm a year in places.

14
00:02:16,030 --> 00:02:26,096
Remember Mexico City as the city that sinks, built on a drained lake, with its
Zócalo over the Aztec ceremonial precinct.

15
00:02:30,612 --> 00:02:42,466
Ottawa sits on the Ottawa river in Ontario, Washington on the Potomac, and
Mexico City in its high valley of volcanoes, two thousand kilometres to the
south.

16
00:02:43,130 --> 00:02:53,625
Remember the trade-route capital chosen for its distance from the American
border, the capital that belongs to no state, and the sinking city built on a
drained lake.
